🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗡𝗼𝗱𝗲 𝗶𝘀 𝗡𝗼𝘁𝗥𝗲𝗮𝗱𝘆 — 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁 𝗜𝘁?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “One of your Kubernetes worker nodes suddenly changes to "NotReady", and Pods running on that node are affected. How will you troubleshoot it?”

In production, I wouldn't immediately reboot the node. First, I'd identify why Kubernetes considers it unhealthy.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗔𝗽𝗽𝗿𝗼𝗮𝗰𝗵

1️⃣ Check node status

kubectl get nodes
kubectl describe node <node-name>

Review node conditions, events, and "Ready" status.

2️⃣ Check resource pressure

kubectl top node
df -h
df -i
free -m

Look for memory pressure, disk pressure, inode exhaustion, or resource exhaustion.

3️⃣ Check Kubelet

On the affected node:

systemctl status kubelet
journalctl -u kubelet --since "30 minutes ago"

Investigate service failures, certificate issues, and communication problems.

4️⃣ Check the container runtime

systemctl status containerd

If your cluster uses containerd, verify that the runtime is healthy and inspect its logs if necessary.

5️⃣ Check networking

Investigate node connectivity, DNS, firewall rules, CNI components, and communication with the Kubernetes API Server.

6️⃣ Check affected workloads

kubectl get pods -A -o wide
kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp

Identify affected Pods and review scheduling or eviction events.

7️⃣ Recover safely

If the node remains unhealthy, follow your incident procedure. Consider cordoning or draining it when appropriate, after evaluating workload availability and disruption risks.

🎯 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

A "NotReady" node is a symptom, not the root cause.

Node Status → Conditions → Resources → Kubelet → Runtime → Networking → Root Cause

Always verify node health and workload recovery after taking corrective action.
